    id: "ai-injection-boundaries", category: "AI", title: "Prompt injection and structured-output trust boundaries",
    summary: "Keep retrieved instructions and model-generated JSON outside the application's authority.",
    what: "Prompt injection attempts to redirect model behavior through untrusted content, including retrieved pages or uploaded documents. Structured output can constrain response shape, but a valid JSON schema does not establish that the proposed action is authorized, true, or appropriate.",
    why: "A model may encounter text that requests data disclosure or unrelated tool use. If the application treats generated instructions as authority, an answer-quality problem can become an external side effect or a privacy failure.",
    how: "Keep instruction and data roles explicit, restrict available tools and retrieved data, and enforce identity, ownership, arguments, and business rules outside the model. Validate generated fields and reject unknown or excessive values. Require appropriate confirmation for consequential actions and bound loops, time, and output. Treat detection as defense in depth, not a complete solution. Test with hostile document fixtures in a non-production environment.",
    flow: "Untrusted material -> bounded context -> model proposal -> schema validation -> independent authorization -> permitted action",
    example: "A retrieved note tells the assistant to export all customer records.\nThe document is evidence, not permission.\nThe application exposes only a narrow caller-scoped search tool,\nand validates account scope before any response or operation.",
    cheat: ["Valid JSON is not a trusted command.", "Prompt text is not an access-control boundary.", "Least-privilege tools reduce possible damage.", "Approval must show the actual consequential action."],
    pitfalls: ["A denylist of suspicious phrases cannot cover every injection.", "Giving read-only tasks write-capable tools expands unnecessary risk."],
    exercise: "Threat-model a document assistant with read and update tools. Add adversarial document tests, independent resource checks, refusal expectations, and sanitized audit evidence.",
