    id: "js-event-loop", category: "JavaScript / TypeScript", title: "Event loop, microtasks, and CPU-bound work",
    summary: "Predict browser callback ordering and keep expensive computation from blocking interaction.",
    what: "A JavaScript job runs to completion on its execution agent. Browsers coordinate tasks, microtask checkpoints, and rendering opportunities. Promise reactions use microtasks; a timer callback is scheduled through the host's task mechanism and is not guaranteed to run at an exact wall-clock delay.",
    why: "A long synchronous operation prevents input handling and rendering even inside an async function. An endless chain of microtasks can also delay other useful work rather than creating fairness automatically.",
    how: "Separate CPU work from asynchronous I/O. Use a worker for suitable heavy computation with an explicit message, cancellation, and ownership contract. Yield deliberately where supported for incremental main-thread work, and do not assume await Promise.resolve permits rendering. Profile real interaction latency. Distinguish the browser model from Node-specific phases instead of generalizing one scheduling example to every host.",
    flow: "Current job -> microtask checkpoint -> next eligible host work/rendering opportunity -> next job",
