    summary: "Preserve relationships between inputs and outputs without treating static types as runtime checks.",
    what: "Generics express relationships between types. keyof describes property keys, indexed access selects a property type, and mapped types transform a set of properties. These tools operate in the static type system rather than validating an incoming runtime object.",
    why: "Returning any from reusable helpers loses the exact relationship the caller needs. Broad casts can conceal a mismatch, while overcomplicated generic APIs can become harder to use than a direct explicit contract.",
    how: "Begin with the relationship the helper must preserve and add the smallest useful constraint. Use a key parameter constrained by keyof T so a property's return type remains T[K]. Apply mapped types for intentional partial or readonly views, noting that common utilities do not automatically recurse into every nested object. Validate unknown external values before calling strongly typed application code.",
