    id: "devops-iac-state", category: "DevOps", title: "Infrastructure as code, state, and drift",
    summary: "Review infrastructure changes as controlled transitions rather than blindly executing a plan.",
    what: "Infrastructure as code describes managed resources in versioned configuration. Terraform state maps those declarations to tracked remote objects and can contain sensitive values. Drift is a difference between the intended or tracked state and the actual environment.",
    why: "Manual changes and concurrent applies can produce surprising plans. A replacement action may destroy data-bearing infrastructure, and a sensitive-marked output is not proof the underlying state contains no secret.",
    how: "Isolate environments and use protected remote state with supported locking and recovery. Run plans with the intended identity, review creates, updates, replacements, and deletions, and apply the approved artifact under controlled permissions. Investigate drift before importing, changing, or replacing resources. Require explicit human approval for destructive changes and verify backups and recovery where data is involved. Keep state files out of public repositories.",
